# Markdown formatting

## File format

- Use `.md` for pages under `docs/`. The site compiles those files as MDX, so React
  components such as `<Tabs>` and `<GlossaryTerm>` still render. This rule doesn't apply
  to root-level files such as `README.md` and `CONTRIBUTING.md`.
- Prefer HTML-like formatting over plain Markdown syntax for elements like tables (use
  `<table>` rather than pipe/dash Markdown tables). Several elements already require HTML
  syntax (`<Tabs>`, images), so using it everywhere avoids mixing both styles on one page.

## Line wrapping

- Default to US English usage.
- When writing prose, wrap at about 100 characters. Start each sentence on its own line.
- Never break words or URLs when wrapping a line.

## Headings

- Don't add a top-level `# Heading` unless you want the rendered page heading to read
  differently from the frontmatter `title` (Docusaurus pulls `title` for the sidebar
  label, not the H1).

## Frontmatter

Every page needs YAML frontmatter with at least `title` and `description`:

```mdx
---
title: Title of the article
description: Description of the article
---
```

Don't add an `image` field manually: the social card is generated automatically on build.

## Links

- The `](/` pattern is an absolute link; `](./` and `](../` are relative links. Prefer
  relative links within the same tab.
- Use descriptive link text embedded in the sentence. Don't use "click here" or a bare
  URL.
- If you move or rename a page, see `.cursor/rules/contributor-workflow.mdc` for the
  `vercel.json` redirect requirement.

## Images

Add new images to `/static/img/`, mirroring the `docs/` folder structure. See
[README.md](../../README.md#adding-images) for the exact JSX pattern and size classes
(`img-xsmall`, `img-small`, `img-medium`, `img-large`).

## UI element references

Use **bold**, not *italics*, to reference a UI element the reader interacts with (a
button, tab, menu item, or field label), for example "Select **Compile**" or "Go to the
**Solidity Compiler** tab." Reserve italics for true emphasis.

## Collapsible content

Wrap optional or supplementary content that would interrupt the main flow of a page (a
file-tree dump, an edge-case troubleshooting note) in a collapsible block instead of
inline text or a deeply nested sub-heading:

```mdx
<details>
<summary>Project structure</summary>
<div>

Content goes here.

</div>
</details>
```

## Markdownlint

`npm run lint:md` checks `docs/**/*.md`. Lines of 200 characters are allowed.
The check's line limit is 600, because some glossary definitions are longer
than 200 and markdownlint does not reflow them.
Keep `<GlossaryTerm>` on the same line as the words around it. A blank line before
the component splits the sentence into two paragraphs.

JavaScript and JSON still run through Prettier on commit. Documentation does not.
